WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.983
Different secrets deserve different handling.

2
00:00:01.983 --> 00:00:09.120
Passwords, API keys, access tokens, refresh tokens, private keys, certificates, database connection strings, signing keys, encryption keys, session

3
00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:12.293
secrets, and service credentials all carry different risk.

4
00:00:12.293 --> 00:00:18.241
A short-lived access token is not the same as a long-lived signing key.

5
00:00:18.241 --> 00:00:23.396
A local development credential is not the same as a production database credential.

6
00:00:23.396 --> 00:00:28.154
Treating all of them as generic configuration values hides important security decisions.

7
00:00:28.154 --> 00:00:30.137
Classification should answer practical questions.

8
00:00:30.137 --> 00:00:32.120
What does this secret allow?

9
00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:33.706
Which system owns it?

10
00:00:33.706 --> 00:00:35.688
Which application component needs it?

11
00:00:35.688 --> 00:00:38.068
Which environment is it valid in?

12
00:00:38.068 --> 00:00:40.050
How long should it live?

13
00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:41.637
Who can rotate it?

14
00:00:41.637 --> 00:00:45.205
What happens if it is suspected to be exposed?

15
00:00:45.205 --> 00:00:53.929
A secret with broad privileges and a long lifetime needs stronger storage, tighter access control, and more deliberate monitoring than a narrowly

16
00:00:53.929 --> 00:00:55.119
scoped temporary credential.

17
00:00:55.119 --> 00:00:58.688
The lifecycle matters because secret handling is rarely static.

18
00:00:58.688 --> 00:01:05.032
Values are created, delivered, loaded, used, refreshed, rotated, revoked, archived in some systems, and eventually deleted.

19
00:01:05.032 --> 00:01:08.601
Planning those steps before an emergency reduces improvisation later.

20
00:01:08.601 --> 00:01:16.532
Documentation should connect the secret type to its approved store, delivery path, runtime owner, rotation process, and incident response expectation.

21
00:01:16.532 --> 00:01:22.480
That gives developers and operators a shared model rather than leaving every caller to guess.
